⏺ I've finished the read-only inspection. Nothing in the
  repo or the toolkit has been created or modified.
     
  1. What already exists

  Build and environment (confirmed in gradle.properties and
  build.gradle): Minecraft 1.20.1, Forge 47.4.28,
  ForgeGradle 6.x, official mappings, Java 17 toolchain,
  JUnit 5.9.3. There are no mod dependencies or extra
  repositories. The git tree is clean on main, with 10
  milestone commits.

  Architecture (about 7,600 lines of Java):

  Area: Entry point
  What's there: LunchtimeJobs registers the config (COMMON
  type), sets up networking in enqueueWork, sets up the
  client through DistExecutor, and registers the three XP
  handlers on the Forge event bus by hand.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: Professions
  What's there: Profession (an immutable builder) and
  ProfessionRegistry (a thread-safe singleton). Farmer,
  Rancher and Chef each have a Profession, a Registry and
  an XpHandler.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: XP engine
  What's there: ProfessionService.awardXp is the only way XP

  is awarded. Checks run in order: client side, amount,
  registered, unlocked, active, max level. Then it applies
  the multiplier (rounded down), fires the
  ProfessionXpEvent.Pre event (which listeners can cancel
  or modify), fires one ProfessionLevelUpEvent per level
  gained, sends chat and sound, syncs, and fires Post.
  ExperienceHelper handles the LINEAR/POLYNOMIAL curves
  with overflow protection.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: Capability
  What's there: IPlayerProfessions with
  @AutoRegisterCapability, schema version 1. Data is copied
   on clone using reviveCaps/invalidateCaps, and the
  capability is invalidated through event.addListener.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: Networking
  What's there: SimpleChannel protocol "1.0.0" with two
  server-to-client packets: the player's own full data as
  NBT, and other players' active professions and levels
  (capped at 128 entries). Sync happens on login, respawn,
  dimension change, start/stop tracking, and every XP
  award.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: Anti-exploit patterns
  What's there: A deferred next-tick verification queue
  (Farmer harvests, Rancher shearing, milking and growth),
  FakePlayer XP off by default, creative/spectator
  excluded, a per-animal milking cooldown, LRU-bounded
  caches, batch XP caps for Chef, a re-entrancy guard,
  queues cleared on ServerStoppingEvent. Melons, pumpkins,
  sugar cane and cactus are deliberately left out of Farmer
   (place-and-break exploit).
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: Data
  What's there: 5 tags (crops, breedable animals, Chef
  foods/advanced/excluded). All of them list vanilla
  entries only.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: Tests
  What's there: 11 JUnit classes. The last recorded run had
  64 tests, all passing. There are 47 GameTests (Farmer 14,
   Rancher 15, Chef 18) under
  @GameTestHolder(lunchtimejobs), plus one template
  empty.nbt.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Area: Integration
  What's there: EmbersQuestingIntegration is only a
  ModList.isLoaded check plus a planning comment.
  
  Claude tooling: none. There's no CLAUDE.md, no .claude/
  directory, no docs/, no CHANGELOG, and auto-memory is
  empty.

  2. What's missing

  - Project knowledge: there's no CLAUDE.md, so every
    session starts from zero. Architecture, anti-exploit
    rules and the reasoning behind decisions exist only in
    Javadoc and commit messages.
  - Profession specs: no written activity specs (event,
    verification method, config key, exclusions, tests) for
    Farmer, Rancher or Chef.
  - Compatibility tracking: no compatibility knowledge base
    and no evidence log.
  - Repeatable workflows: none for verification,
    compatibility research or release checks, and no
    CHANGELOG to check against.
  - Safeguards: no permission rules or hooks of any kind.
  - Verification evidence: run/logs/latest.log comes from a
    client run at 17:09, which overwrote any earlier
    GameTest log. I found no saved evidence that
    runGameTestServer passed after the Chef milestone. That
    doesn't mean it failed; it means nothing proves it
    passed.

  3. Proposed directory structure

  CLAUDE.md                         ~120 lines: identity, 
  commands, invariants, doc map, scope rules
  CHANGELOG.md                      new; release-check 
  maintains it (back-filled from milestone commits)
  .claude/
    settings.json                   permissions + hooks 
  (committed)
    hooks/
      guard-bash.sh                 blocks push/destructive 
  git, catches compound commands
      protect-tests.sh              blocks edits that delete
  test files or reduce @Test/@GameTest count
    rules/                          path-scoped, load only 
  when matching files are touched
      forge-java.md                 src/main/java/**: 
  sidedness, server authority, handler conventions
      professions.md                profession/**, 
  ModConfig.java: activity checklist, gate order
      networking-client.md          networking/**, client/**
      capability.md                 player/**
      testing.md                    src/test/**, gametest/**
      data-resources.md             src/main/resources/**: 
  tags, lang, mods.toml
      build-deps.md                 build.gradle, 
  gradle.properties, settings.gradle
    skills/
      new-profession/      SKILL.md + design-template.md
      mod-compat-audit/    SKILL.md + mod-entry-template.md
      forge-event-research/SKILL.md
      xp-exploit-audit/    SKILL.md + checklist.md
      verify-mod/          SKILL.md
      release-check/       SKILL.md
    agents/
      forge-architect.md, mod-compatibility-researcher.md,
      multiplayer-exploit-auditor.md, testing-engineer.md
    agent-memory/                   project-scoped 
  specialist memory (created by agents)
  docs/
    README.md                       index: what each doc 
  covers, when to read it
    architecture/   overview.md, profession-system.md, 
  xp-engine.md,
                    networking-sync.md, 
  capability-lifecycle.md
    professions/    farmer.md, rancher.md, chef.md   
  (activity definitions)
    anti-exploit.md
    testing.md
    compatibility/  README.md (statuses + evidence rules), 
  matrix.md, mods/<modid>.md
    research/       standards.md, findings-log.md   (source 
  + version for each discovery)
    decisions/      ADR-0001… (server authority, deferred 
  verification, FakePlayer default,
                    multiplier semantics, gourd/cane 
  exclusion)

  CLAUDE.md only points to docs/ and never imports it, so
  the large docs load only when a rule, skill or agent asks
  for them.

  4. Proposed skills

  Skill: new-profession
  How it runs: You invoke it; main thread
  Responsibility: Fills out a design doc in
  docs/professions/<id>.md covering all 10 required
  sections. It traces events using forge-event-research and
   has to produce an implementation plan before any Java
  file is edited. It stops for your approval.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: mod-compat-audit
  How it runs: Forked to the mod-compatibility-researcher
  agent
  Responsibility: Finds the exact JAR in the canonical pack
  and inspects it with unzip -l and javap -p  -c from the
  JDK, plus source repositories. It records version,
  classes, methods, events, attribution, cancellation and
  automation behaviour, each with evidence. It returns a
  draft mods/<modid>.md, and it is forbidden to mark
  anything above "Investigating" without evidence.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: forge-event-research
  How it runs: Forked, read-only
  Responsibility: Traces the actual call path in the Forge
  47.4.28 and Minecraft sources from the ForgeGradle cache
  (for example ResultSlot → ItemCraftedEvent). It answers
  "does this event mean the action succeeded?" with file
  and line citations and logs each discovery.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: xp-exploit-audit
  How it runs: Forked to the auditor agent, read-only
  Responsibility: Runs checklist.md (duplicate XP,
  automation, FakePlayers, creative/spectator, repeated
  interactions, attribution, races, leaks, performance)
  against a handler or diff. It outputs findings with
  severity and does not edit anything.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: verify-mod
  How it runs: Main thread
  Responsibility: Runs ./gradlew clean test build and
  ./gradlew runGameTestServer, saves the logs, and parses
  the JUnit XML plus the GameTest summary. It reports each
  level on its own line: Compiled / Unit tested / GameTest
  passed / Manually tested (always "not done" unless you
  confirm). Compiling is never reported as gameplay
  verification.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: release-check
  How it runs: You invoke it
  47.4.28 and Minecraft sources from the ForgeGradle cache
  (for example ResultSlot → ItemCraftedEvent). It answers
  "does this event mean the action succeeded?" with file
  and line citations and logs each discovery.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: xp-exploit-audit
  How it runs: Forked to the auditor agent, read-only
  Responsibility: Runs checklist.md (duplicate XP,
  automation, FakePlayers, creative/spectator, repeated
  interactions, attribution, races, leaks, performance)
  against a handler or diff. It outputs findings with
  severity and does not edit anything.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: verify-mod
  How it runs: Main thread
  Responsibility: Runs ./gradlew clean test build and
  ./gradlew runGameTestServer, saves the logs, and parses
  the JUnit XML plus the GameTest summary. It reports each
  level on its own line: Compiled / Unit tested / GameTest
  passed / Manually tested (always "not done" unless you
  confirm). Compiling is never reported as gameplay
  verification.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Skill: release-check
  How it runs: You invoke it
  Responsibility: Checks git diff scope against the
  milestone, makes sure dependencies are unchanged,
  compares test counts with the previous milestone, checks
  config keys against the docs, compatibility statuses and
  CHANGELOG, then runs verify-mod. The result is a go/no-go
   report.
  5. Proposed agents
  Agent: forge-architect
  Tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, WebFetch, WebSearch
  Writes?: No. It advises and the main thread implements,
  which keeps design review separate from editing.
  Memory: project
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Agent: mod-compatibility-researcher
  Tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash (unzip/javap into the
  scratchpad only), WebFetch, WebSearch
  Writes?: No. It returns findings and the main thread
  updates docs/.
  Memory: project (mod versions and class locations it has
  already worked out)
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Agent: multiplayer-exploit-auditor
  Tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash (git diff/log)
  Writes?: No
  Memory: project (known exploit patterns)
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Agent: testing-engineer
  Tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, Edit, Write
  Writes?: Only under src/test/** and gametest/**. That
  limit
  is in its instructions and backed up by the protect-tests
   hook.
  Memory: project

  Things to know about these agents:
  - Bash permissions: Bash rules can't be scoped per agent.
    Project deny rules and hooks apply to every agent.
  - Format check before writing: I'll check the current
    frontmatter fields (tools, memory, context: fork, paths:
    for rules) against the Claude Code docs.

  Safeguards for settings.json:
  - Deny:
    - git push, reset --hard, clean, restore, checkout -- ,
      branch -D, rebase, commit --amend, rm -rf.
    - The guard hook also blocks these when they're chained
      inside a compound command, which pattern rules alone
      can miss.
  - Ask:
    - git commit.
    - Edits to build.gradle, gradle.properties,
      settings.gradle and mods.toml.
  - Unrelated source changes: these can't be blocked
    reliably, so release-check flags them by comparing the
    diff against the declared scope. 
  - No bypass mode, no MCP servers, no plugins: none are
    proposed.
  - No new tools needed: javap from JDK 17 covers inspecting
    mod signatures. A decompiler such as Vineflower would
    only help for reading method bodies. That would be a
    local tool outside the project, and I'd ask before 
    adding it.

  6. Compatibility knowledge extracted

  No mod list is in the repo, so I looked read-only at your
  CurseForge instances. Two are on Forge 47.4.28:
  - "Lunchtime Cozy Create and Cooking (1)" has 379 JARs.
  - "(2)" has 371 JARs.

  They differ. Only (1) has Connector, FastFurnace,
  FastWorkbench and FastSuite. Only (2) has Create
  Numismatics and CreateLazyTick.

  Mods that bear directly on existing code. Every one of
  these is Planned, and none has been verified:

  Mod (JAR version): JobsPlus 1.3.10 + JobsPlusTools
  Why it matters: A competing jobs mod. It may also register

  /jobs, which would clash with JobsCommand. That clash
  needs checking, plus how the two would coexist.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): RightClickHarvest 4.6.1
  Why it matters: Directly affects Farmer right-click
  harvesting. If it cancels RightClickBlock, our
  LOW-priority handler (which skips cancelled events) never
   awards XP.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): Farmer's Delight 1.3.4 + ~8 Delight
  addons
  Why it matters: The cooking pot, skillet and cutting board
  
  probably don't fire ItemSmeltedEvent, so Chef XP would be
   missed. Rice and tomato crop classes may not extend
  CropBlock.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): Kaleidoscope Cookery 1.6.0, Tavern
  1.2.0, + 8 addons
  Why it matters: These have their own cooking blocks. The
  output-taking path needs tracing.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): Create 6.0.8 + Central Kitchen,
  Enchantment Industry, …
  Why it matters: Deployers, harvesters and mechanical
  crafters. They should be denied by the FakePlayer and
  real-player checks, which needs confirming.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): FastWorkbench / FastFurnace (pack 1)
  Why it matters: They replace vanilla container logic,
  which
  affects whether the crafting and smelting events fire and
   how many times.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): Connector beta.49 (pack 1)
  Why it matters: Fabric mods running under it may not fire
  Forge events.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): Ember's Questing 1.2.0
  Why it matters: The stub targets "1.1.0+". The API names
  QuestApi and QuestEvents in its Javadoc are unverified.
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Mod (JAR version): Puffish Skills, Let's Do series, Alex's

  Mobs/Delight, Fishermen's Trap, Starcatcher Delight, Easy
   Villagers, Goblin Traders, Supplementaries, Storage
  Drawers, Tom's Storage, Gentle Seasons
  Why it matters: These go in the matrix by category,
  starting at Planned.

  7. Conflicts and outdated instructions

  1. No CLAUDE.md exists. The task refers to a current one,
     but there's nothing to preserve.
  2. README drift:
     - It describes progression/ as "milestones, perks,
       rewards", but that package holds the XP service.
     - It says packets are "bidirectional", but both are
       server-to-client.
     - registry/, data/, hud/, screens/ and rendering/
       contain only a package-info file each.
     - It says Ember's Questing "1.1.0+", but the pack has
       1.2.0.
     - It doesn't mention the three professions or GameTests
       at all.
  3. Ember's Questing Javadoc: it mentions "Miner" and
     "Blacksmith" professions that don't exist, and API
     names nobody has verified.
  4. Milestone 3 commit message: it says "modded crop tags",
     but farmer_harvestable_crops.json contains only
     vanilla entries.
  5. defaultMaxLevel config: it is only read as a fallback
     in JobsCommand. Profession.Builder hard-codes 100.
  6. perProfessionXpMultipliers: an override replaces the
     global multiplier instead of being multiplied by it,
     while the Javadoc says it is "taking into account"
     both. I'll write this up as an ADR with an open
     question for you, and I won't change the behaviour.
  7. Chef duplicate check: markEventProcessed keys on an
     integer event hash, so two unrelated events could
     collide and one would lose its XP. That's a candidate
     for the first xp-exploit-audit to confirm; I'm not
     fixing it here.
  8. No saved GameTest evidence after Milestone 5, as noted
     in section 2.
  9. ResourceLocation.fromNamespaceAndPath: this is newer
     API. It compiles on 47.4.28, so I'll record that as
     "verified by compilation" in case anyone copies 1.20.1
     tutorials that use new ResourceLocation(...).

